  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve as the difference, in percentage points of valid votes, between the first- and second-place candidates in the Brazlian national presidential election held on October 25, 2026, published by Brazil’s [Tribunal Superior Eleitoral (TSE)](https://www.tse.jus.br/eleicoes/informacoes-tecnicas-sobre-a-divulgacao-de-resultados).\n\nThe value will be calculated as: winner’s valid-vote share − runner-up’s valid-vote share. For example, if the winner receives 52.4% of valid votes and the runner-up receives 47.6%, the question resolves to 4.8 percentage points.",
  "fine_print": "[The TSE](https://www.tse.jus.br/comunicacao/noticias/2026/Marco/eleicoes-2026-confira-as-principais-datas-do-calendario-eleitoral) defines valid votes as votes cast for candidates, excluding blank and null ballots. The final nationwide TSE presidential result will be used including any corrected official figures.",
  "background": "Brazil held the first round of its 2026 general election on October 4, 2026. No presidential candidate won an absolute majority of valid votes. With t[he vast majority of the votes counted](https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/05/brazil-presidential-race-election-result-second-round-lula-flavio-bolsonaro) the President, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva won 45.1% (53.8m votes) whilst his Liberal Party challenger, Flávio Bolsonaro won 47.0% (56.1m votes). The second-round runoff will be held on October 25, 2026.\n\nThe presidential contest is forecasted to be highly competitve. The victory margin can imply very different political environments irrespective of the eventual winner. The runoff margin can therefore serve as a compact indicator of electoral polarization, the breadth of the eventual winner’s coalition, and the degree to which the electorate remains evenly divided at the end of the campaign.\n\nA [Datafolha poll](https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/lula-maintains-lead-over-flavio-bolsonaro-ahead-brazil-election-datafolha-poll-2026-09-17/) on September 17 reported Lula at 46% and Bolsonaro at 44% in a hypothetical second-round matchup, while [other recent polls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_for_the_2026_Brazilian_presidential_election) have also shown narrow margins and variation in which candidate holds a numerical lead. The runoff margin can move materially during the remaining campaign because of candidate debates, endorsements, economic news, campaign strategy, first-round vote transfers, and changes in turnout among different voter blocs.",
  "nr_forecasters": 57,
